#N canvas 20 20 3000 2200 12;
#X declare -lib pdlua;
#X text 20 20 c_steps -- a 32-step sequencer: sixteen tracks of 32 steps \, each with its own last step \, a velocity per step \, played on its own c_clock. It knows steps \, velocity levels and pads \, never where they sit on a surface: that is the m_ boundary. ref/module/sequencer.md, f 58;
#X text 20 150 creation arg 1: its bank \, 1 for A \, vestigial since the SP-404 left the rig (2026-11-04): track n is pad (bank-1)x16+n and c_kit plays 1 to 16 \, so only bank 1 sounds. It stays because dropping it would move the part \, arg 2 \, and the state key named for it. creation arg 2: the part its pattern is stored as in a section \, drums. inlet: step \, vel \, pad and rep <n> <velocity> \, a press or a release \, last \, track-last and swing <0-1> from knobs 2 to 4 \, and the phone's controls \, c_steps.lua's R.presses. outlet 1: pad <1..160> <velocity> \, and vol \, pitch and each effect <pad> <value> \, and the reverb's size and damping <value> \, and a held repeat's tape <span> <units> and tape-off \, to c_kit. outlet 2: the drum page's state \, to g_grid -- steps <32 levels> \, play <step> \, vel <level> \, track <pad> \, last <n> \, bar <steps> \, rep <pad> \, fast and recall <1 or 0> \, quiet <16 flags> \, 1 for a track that won't play. Knobs 2 and 3 draw their rows on disp from here. outlet 3: the phone's state \, to u_net \, 148 numbers whenever it changes. outlet 4: bar \, on the global loop's last step \, to u_song: a bar is the pattern's loop \, and recall <key> \, a part's saved value asked for. outlet 5: fast <1 or 0> \, the 500 BPM repeat's tempo \, to u_tempo. The tempo bus's BPM goes to the Lua with each tick \, not as it changes: a sweep of knob 1 is some 200 a second. It keeps it with the step \, for a held repeat's tails., f 58;
