#X text 20 1820 Steps are cut from the sequencer's own phase \, never from c_clock's beat bang \, which tops out at 14.3 a second (ref/module/tempo.md). The phase runs at two steps a cycle \, at c_clock's rate times the Lua's rate: rzero~ and wrap~ read c_clock's phase as an increment per sample \, the expr~ drops the jump of a start \, and samplerate~ turns it back into a frequency. At rate 1 it is c_clock's beat phase times two \, so a step lands where it did. Drunk \, the Lua sets a rate for every step \, and the drift stays: nothing pulls the phase back but PLAY. Swing bends each pair: samphold~ takes the swing at the pair's start \, so a turn never moves a step already cut \, and the second expr~ gives the first step the swing's share of the pair and the second the rest. Then times two \, wrap~ \, and a threshold at the boundary: the -~ shifts it back a ten-thousandth of a pair so PLAY's reset re-arms threshold~ \, and step 1 fires within two blocks (ref/module/sequencer.md). Debounces 0 \, as in c_clock., f 58;
#X text 20 2010 Each track keeps its own place in its own loop \, in the Lua: a tick moves every one on \, and a track wraps after its own last step. Per-track subdivision would give a track its own multiplier on the same phase in place of the times four., f 58;
#X text 300 670 PLAY starts every loop at step 1: start tells the Lua \, before the spigot shuts for one logical instant \, so a step cut before c_clock's phase reset cannot fire as step 1. STOP and panic shut it \, and stop tells the Lua \, which puts the playhead out., f 44;
#X text 520 1100 On each step the Lua answers tick with every track not at 0 on its own step \, or as its randomness flips it \, or while a repeat pad is held \, as the step repeated played \, but none muted or left out of a solo \, in one logical instant \, and c_kit plays them all: a voice per pad. A mute stops a track's hits here \, so its echoes and reverb ring out in c_kit. Then the playhead moves \, to the selected track's step., f 44;
#X text 1000 1450 The rules live in c_steps.lua: the pattern \, the step rule \, the velocity levels \, the selected track and level \, the last steps and the knobs' latch \, each track's place in its loop \, and which tracks fire on a step. c_steps-lua is its Pd face. This file keeps the clock \, the transport \, the cords to c_kit \, g_grid and u_net \, and the state bus. Edit the rules there \, not here: test/gate/lua/c_steps_test.lua runs them without Pd. A press the Lua does not know is fail c_steps bad-selector \, and a Lua error fail c_steps lua-<selector> (C-12)., f 50;
#X text 1600 1500 The pattern is the playing section's part <part> \, put with the section policy \, which the song store keeps (ref/module/songs.md): 512 velocities \, track by track \, then the sixteen tracks' own last steps \, their randomness \, volumes and pitches \, the time signature \, the drunkenness \, the swing and the global last step \, then once any is used each track's effects and the reverb's \, and once any track is muted or soloed each track's mute and solo. The Lua takes a section's restore \, or u_state's of section-1-<part> \, the auto key it was before sections \, once \, and answers with the pattern \, which is put straight back \, and with every track's volume and pitch for c_kit: a key nobody re-puts is dropped \, and a short or malformed line is repaired by the same put., f 40;
#X text 2100 1450 At load the Lua answers init with the page's selection and a bang on its fifth outlet \, which shuts the spigot. No bang means it never loaded -- pdlua missing \, or an error in the Lua -- and fail c_steps no-lua says so on the screen \, the only place a device with no console can., f 40;
